## ABSTRACT
A Ni/Co metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and its reduced graphene oxide (rGO) composite has been fabricated through room-temperature route using simple 3D linker as trimesic acid. The crystalline nature, morphology, functional groups and thermal stability of the prepared materials are characterized via XRD, SEM, Raman and TGA studies. The synthesized Ni/Co-MOF/rGO reveals spherical particles decorated rod-like morphology with a length of ${\sim}3{\cdot}10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The incorporation of rGO into the Ni/Co-MOF enhances conductivity and electrochemical performance. The electrochemical properties of Ni/Co-MOF and its rGO composite are evaluated in 1 M KOH. The produced Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite $(1162\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1})$ ) exhibits higher specific capacitance than bare Ni/Co-MOF $(978\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1})$ ). The Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite exposes excellent stability even after 5000 cycles and maintains $109\%$ of its initial capacitance. This result suggests that the fabricated Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite may be employed as an electrode material for supercapacitor applications.
Keywords: Metal-organic frameworks; Carbon nanomaterials; energy storage; Bimetallic;   
electrochemistry.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, researchers much focused on energy devices, due to increasing energy demand and diminishing fossil fuel [1]. Supercapacitors received tremendous attraction and hopeful technologies for future energy storage [2]. It possesses interesting properties, for instance, extraordinary power density, small charging period and long cycle life which generate them a suitable candidate for applications for example frequency regulation in smart grids, hybrid electric vehicles, pulse power applications and portable electronic devices [3,4,5]. Based on the energy storage mechanism, supercapacitors categorized into two types: electrical doublelayer capacitors (EDLCs) and pseudocapacitors. Electrochemical supercapacitors supply electrical energy via accumulation charge at electrode/electrolyte interfaces (non-faradaic), pseudocapacitors can supply energy through charge transfer redox reaction occurring at the electrode surface [6,7,8]
Porous and nanostructured metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) built by the association of metal ions that act as lattice nodes and multidentate organic linkers which are received much attention on account of its high surface area, tailorable architectures, topologies and tunable pore size [9,10]. Owing to their robust nature, thermal and chemical stability it has been extensively utilized in potential applications such as sensors [11], catalysis [12], batteries [13], gas separation [14], optoelectronics [15], molecular sieving [16] and drug delivery [17]. There are four different approaches for supercapacitor application of MOFs based material: (1) Pristine MOF as such utilized as electrode material [18]; (2) MOFs as sacrificial template for the synthesis of metal oxide [19]; (3) MOFs derived metal nanoparticles [20]; (4) Pyrolysed at high temperatures yields porous carbon compounds [21]. Compared to entire approaches, MOFs directly employed as an active material for supercapacitors are not studied as much. Since the conductivity of the pristine
MOFs is very pity, and d-orbital participation of metal ions with organic linker thwarts the effective delocalization of electrons across the framework [22].
In recent times, carbon-based materials for instance graphite oxide [23], graphene [24], reduced graphene oxide [25] and CNTs [26] have been successfully utilized for supercapacitor application due to its intriguing properties long cycle life, high surface area, superior chemical stability and remarkable electrical conductivity [27]. H. Feng et al. prepared the rGO using lowtemperature technique and found that the prepared rGO unveils a specific capacitance value of $263\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ without any binder [28]. J.-D. Nam studied the all-solid-state rGO supercapacitor and reported a higher specific volumetric capacitance value of 108 F $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ [29]. However, the intrinsic limitation of the surface charging mechanism and agglomeration or restacking of carbon nanomaterials intensely diminishes the active surface area available to the electrolyte, results in the lesser capacitive behavior. To prevent the agglomeration of carbon nanomaterials, guest materials are embedded [30]. P. Srimuk et al. investigated the rGO/HKUST-1 coated on carbon fiber paper and indicated the specific capacitance value of $385\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ [31]. M. Saraf et al. examined the supercapacitor performance of Cu-MOF/rGO hybrid and stated the high storage capacitance of $685.33\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at 1.6 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [32]. Y. Zhang et al. inspected the CNTs $@$ Mn-MOF based symmetric supercapacitor and revealed that the fabricated electrode shows the specific capacitance of $203.1\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 3000 cycles [33].
In this research, we effectively produced the Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite via a simplistic room temperature method and characterized by spectroscopic and microscopic techniques. The prepared material was cast on nickel foil and studied their charge storage performance in $^{1\mathrm{~M~}}$ KOH solution.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Graphite powder $99\%$ , Alfa Aesar, APS 7-11 micron), ${\bf N a N O}_{3}$ $299\%$ , Merck), ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ 1 $98\%$ , Fisher Scientific), $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ $99\%$ , SRL), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $35\%$ , Alfa Aesar), HCl $35{-}38\%$ , Thomas Baker) and de-ionized water were utilized in the preparation of graphite oxide. $\mathrm{N_{2}H_{4}}.\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ $98+\%$ , Alfa Aesar), ethanol $99\%$ , Hayman) were utilized in the synthesis of reduced graphene oxide. $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $99\%$ , Spectrum), $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $99.9\%$ , Alfa Aesar), trimesic acid $(95\%$ , Sigma Aldrich), $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{3}\mathrm{N}$ $(\geq99\%$ , Sigma Aldrich), $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{NO}\ \mathrm{(}\geq99.8\%$ , Sigma Aldrich) and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (HPLC grade, Sigma Aldrich) were utilized in synthesis of MOF-rGO composite.
## 2.2. Preparation of graphite oxide (GO) and reduced graphene oxide (rGO)
Graphite oxide (GO) was produced via the modified Hummers method [34,35]. Graphite oxide $(1.5\ {\mathrm{g}})$ and de-ionized water ( $\mathrm{500~ml}$ ) were taken in RB flask and sonicated well for 30 minutes. To this hydrazine monohydrate ( $1\upmu\mathrm{l}$ for $3~\mathrm{mg}$ of GO) was mixed well and immersed in an oil bath then heated at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. The consequent black suspension was centrifuged and repeatedly washed with de-ionized water $(1.5\mathrm{L})$ and then with EtOH $(500~\mathrm{ml})$ ). The product was dried in a vacuum oven at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. Figure 1. Depicts the photographs of the production process of GO, rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite.
## 2.3. Preparation of Ni/Co-MOF (NCM) and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO (NCM-rGO) composite
Metal-organic frameworks and their composite were synthesized according to the previous literature reported by O.M. Yaghi. It was slightly altered through a metal ion solution and pH [36,37,38]. $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\ensuremath{(1.23\mathrm{\g})}$ , $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.82\ \mathrm{g})$ and trimesic acid $(1\ \mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in $180~\mathrm{ml}$ of 1:1:1 ratio of EtOH/DMF/H2O solvent. To this reduced graphene oxide $(0.30\mathrm{~g~}10\mathrm{~wt}\%)$ was added and sonicated well for 30 minutes and then triethylamine was added dropwise manner up to $\mathrm{\pH}$ of the solution was 7 and allowed stirred for 24 hours. The resulting precipitate was filtered and repeatedly washed with DMF solvent and immersed in HPLC grade dichloromethane for overnight. The next day, again washed and immersed in dichloromethane for overnight. The subsequent product was centrifuged and dried in a vacuum oven at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours. An identical procedure followed to synthesize the Ni/Co-MOF (NCM) except the rGO addition to the precursor solution. The resultant product was pale pink solid.
## 2.4. Instrumentation
The prepared MOF and its composite material were confirmed employing Bruker D8 advance XRD furnished with Cu Kα radiation (wavelength: $0.15406~\mathrm{nm}$ ) as the $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray source. The surface morphology prepared materials were investigated by a Tescan scanning electron microscope provided with Vega3sem software. The elemental composition present in the material calculated with Oxford instruments EDAX. The thermal stability of the produced materials was recorded with NETZSCH instruments in a nitrogen flow atmosphere with a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . The functional groups present in the materials were analyzed with Bruker-Tensor 27 series spectrophotometer and Renishaw Invia Laser Raman microscope. The particle size of the synthesized materials was studied with Tecnai 20 G2 (FEI make) Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM). Electrochemical measurements were executed through the CHI 660B electrochemical workstation (CHI instruments, USA).
## 2.5. Electrode fabrication
The electrochemical properties of prepared NCM and NCM-rGO composite materials were assessed in a standard three-electrode arrangement in an aqueous 1 M KOH solution. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and chronopotentiometry (CP) methods were utilized to perform the electrochemical features. The platinum wire, saturated calomel electrode and active materials coated on nickel foil were used as the counter, reference, and working electrodes respectively. The working electrode was fabricated by grinding $80\mathrm{wt}\%$ of active material, $10\mathrm{wt}\%$ of carbon black and $10\mathrm{wt}\%$ of polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) in an agate mortar to form a homogeneous slurry. The resultant slurry was cast on a nickel foil by using the doctor blade method and dried for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a hot air oven. The mass loadings of NCM and NCM-rGO composite materials are approximately 0.7 and $1\:\mathrm{mg}$ respectively. The specific capacitance of the fabricated electrode was estimated from cyclic voltammetric and chronopotentiometric measurements by the following equations (1) and (2), respectively [39].
$$
\begin{array}{r}{C s p=\frac{\int i d V}{S.\Delta V.m}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{C s p=\frac{I\Delta t}{m\Delta V}}\end{array}
$$
Where ∫idV is the integral area of the CV curve, $\Delta V$ is the potential window (V), $m$ is the mass of the active material $\mathrm{(mg)}$ , S is the scan rate $\mathrm{{(mV~s^{-1})}}$ ), $I$ is the discharge current density (A) and $\Delta t$ is the discharge time (s).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Physico-chemical characterization of NCM and NCM-rGO composite
The powder XRD patterns of the synthesized rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite were represented in Fig. 2. It can be observed from the inset figure that the broad peak at $2\uptheta=25.2^{\circ}$ with the d-spacing value of $0.356~\mathrm{nm}$ indicates the (002) plane of the rGO. In the meantime, the PXRD pattern of the Ni/Co-MOF exhibits well defined sharp peaks and consistent with the reported literature [40,41,42,43]. The hybrid composite shows identical peaks with the relatively low intensity which illustrates the amorphous structure of rGO persists in the Ni/CoMOF/rGO composite. The rGO peak overlapped with the MOF material and so the characteristic peaks of Ni/Co-MOF only perceived. There is no peak position shift detected in the hybrid composite, this may recommend that the formation of composite occurs via weak electrostatic interaction [9].
Figure 3 portrays the FT-IR spectra of rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite. The broad band perceived around $3450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ confirms the existence of a hydroxyl functional group. The peak assigned at $1613~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ relates to the deformation of O-H to the C-OH group (coordinated water molecules). The asymmetric and symmetric vibrations of (COO-) group detected at 1517 and $1430~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ respectively. The characteristic frequency of C-O stretching vibration allotted at $1368~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The peak noticed at $728~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ matches with out-ofplane ring bending vibration.
A Raman spectrum of prepared Ni/Co-MOF was plotted in Fig. 4A. The sharp peaks identified in 1612, 1564 and $1475~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ connected with $\mathrm{C=C}$ of the benzene ring, asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of the carboxylate group. The less intense band at $1365~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ indicates the in-plane vibrational modes of the C-O bond. A couple of peaks spotted at 824 and 797 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ relate to the C-H bending and out-of-plane aromatic ring bending vibrations respectively. Fig. 4B shows the D and G bands of rGO and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite. The D and G band originates from the breathing mode of $\upkappa$ -point phonons of $\mathbf{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}$ symmetry and firstorder scattering of $\mathrm{E}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ phonons of $\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathrm{sp}}^{2}$ -hybridized carbon bond respectively [44,45]. Raman spectrum of rGO exhibits two clear vibrational peaks at 1348 (D band) and $1595~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (G band). Similarly Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite displays peaks at 1359 and $1602~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The observed shift in G band by $7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from 1595 (rGO) to $1602~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (MOF-rGO composite) reveals the charge transfer from rGO to Ni/Co-MOF. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratio of the composite was lower than the rGO. It is owing to the emerging of oxygen groups from MOF material and consequently, the decrease in the $\displaystyle\operatorname{sp}^{2}$ domains upon composite formation.
Figure 5 depicts the TG curves of the rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite. The rGO shows preliminary weight loss owing to the removal of physisorbed water. The gradual weight loss starts at nearby $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which can be ascribed to the elimination of labile oxygen functional groups. The first weight loss of the Ni/Co-MOF occurred from 80 to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and is ascribed to the elimination of coordinated water molecules. The second weight loss happened in 415 to $480^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ relates to the disintegration of framework moiety. In the circumstances of Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite, exclusion of physisorbed water starts at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and decomposition of framework architecture around the same temperature as Ni/Co-MOF with the weight loss of $44.6\%$ . These results support the strong interaction between the rGO and Ni/Co-MOF matrix.
The surface morphology of the rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite was exposed in Fig. 6. It can be understood from Fig. 6A that the rGO sheets were flake-like wrinkled structure and overlapped with each other. Fig. 6B demonstrates the SEM image of the MOF crystals and exhibits, rod-like morphology with etched edges. Fig. 6C shows the spherical particles decorated rectangular rod-like morphology for Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite. The length $(3-10~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ ) and thickness $(1-2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ of the rods were varied. As evident from the figure that the rGO particles were strongly intertwined and entrenched in the MOF matrix, the rods were uniformly distributed and randomly oriented. The elemental composition present in the prepared material investigated with EDS as illustrated in Fig. 6D, E and F. The rGO shows the C and O peaks and lower content of oxygen was observed which indicates the reduced form of graphene oxide. Fig. 6E and F display the existence of C, O, Co and Ni peaks. The Co and Ni ratio was approximately 3:2 and consistent with the practical molar ratio.
Figure 7 represents the transmission electron micrographs of the rGO, Ni/Co-MOF, and Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite and their corresponding SAED patterns were shown in insets. The rGO sheets were randomly folded, lateral corrugation and wrinkles to continuous silk wave appearance (Fig. 7A). The SAED pattern of the rGO exhibits diffused rings which confirmed the amorphous nature and disordered structure. The pristine Ni/Co-MOF shows aggregated microparticles (Fig. 7B) and the SAED image reveals multiple diffraction lines denote the polycrystalline nature. The Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite demonstrates the aggregated structure and MOF particles were randomly dispersed and wrapped by the scrolled rGO sheets (Fig. 7C). The rGO sheets connected the different MOF particles and improve the electronic conductivity. The SAED pattern of composite exposes a mixture of crystalline as well as amorphous nature.
Figure 8 exposes the deconvoluted XP spectra of the components present in the Ni/CoMOF/rGO composite. Fig. 8A describes the wide-scan C 1s spectrum. It exhibits four peaks at 283.5, 285.0, 288.4 and 289.2 eV were allotted to C-C, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ , O- $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{{C=O}}}$ and COOH groups respectively [46]. Less intense peaks suggest that the lower quantity of oxygen functionalities (hydroxyl, carbonyl, and carboxylic groups) exists in the composite. Likewise, the most intense peak indicates the predominant character of the aromatic moiety. The O 1s spectrum depicts peaks at 530.4, 531.3 and $532.3\mathrm{eV}$ are associated with metal-oxygen bond, chemisorbed oxygen or coordinated lattice oxygen and $0.0{=}0$ group respectively (Fig. 8B) [47]. The Co 2p spectrum portrays two spin-orbit doublets (780.1 and 795. 5 eV) and satellite peaks (784.5 and 800.1 eV) [48]. The binding energy difference between two doublets and doublets-satellite peaks were 15.4 and $4.4{\cdot}4.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 8C). It is confirmed from the outcomes that the Co element existing in the $+2$ oxidation state as well as in oxide form. Fig. 8D represents the core level scan of Ni 2p spectrum, which reveals peaks at 854.3 and $871.8\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\eV}}$ allocated to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ respectively [49]. The binding energy difference between Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ was $17.5\mathrm{eV}$ and two shakeup satellite peaks were appearing at 859.6 and $877.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ . It is evidenced from the results that the nickel exists in an oxide form and the $+2$ oxidation state.
## 3.2. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical behavior of NCM and NCM-rGO composite is first assessed by CV technique at diverse sweep rate ranges from 5 to $100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ and investigated within the potential window of 0 to 0.5 V vs. SCE (Fig. 9A and B). Interestingly, redox peaks are detected in all CV curves, which indicate the pseudocapacitive nature of the NCM and NCM-rGO composite [50]. This can be attributed to M−O/M−O−OH redox pair $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ and $\mathrm{Co}$ ) and consistent with the intercalation and deintercalation mechanism of $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion during the electrochemical redox process [51].
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}+O H^{-}+H_{2}O\rightarrow N i O O H+2C o O O H+2e^{-}}}}\\ {{\mathrm{CoOOH+OH^{-}\leftrightarrow C o O_{2}+H_{2}O+e^{-}}}}\end{array}
$$
Furthermore, the peak current elevates almost linearly with an increase in the sweep rate suggests good electronic and ionic transport property of the electrode. [52]. Upon increasing the sweep rate, the anodic and cathodic peak potentials are shifted towards more positive and negative regions respectively, which may be ascribed to the excellent polarization effect of NCM and NCM-rGO composite [40]. The shape characteristics of CV curves do not alter even at a high sweep rate of $100\ \mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}$ , implying that high reversible redox reactions, fast charge transport and good kinetic reversibility of the electrode [53].
Fig. 9C displays the CV curves of both NCM and NCM-rGO composite electrodes at a sweep rate of ${5\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}}$ . The NCM-rGO composite electrode exhibits a large integrated area under the CV curve than the NCM, which designates high specific capacitance properties of the NCM-rGO composite electrode. The specific capacitance of electrodes is calculated based on the CV curve via equation 1. Interestingly, the NCM and NCM-rGO electrodes are delivered a maximum specific capacitance value of 678 and 1162 F g respectively at a low scan rate of 5 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}\mathrm{\s}^{-1}$ . From the above results, it is confirmed that the NCM-rGO exhibits superior capacitance than the NCM electrode and comparatively higher specific capacitance than metal-organic frameworks that have been reported in the previous works as given in Table 1. This might have ascribed to the following structural properties: (1) the rod-like morphology exhibits high surface area which provides an electrical pathway for electron transport, facilitates the conduction of electrons, shortens the diffusion path length of ions and provides more pathways for ions diffusion. (2) the complete hybridization of rod-like NCM and electronically conducting rGO exhibits a superior synergetic effect, which affords fast and efficient charge transport character and leading to an increase in the overall electronic conductivity. Fig. 9D shows the specific capacitance versus scan rate and displays an inverse relationship. At the lower sweep rates, the ions in solution are simply diffuse and migrate into the active materials, which give rise to higher capacitance. At higher sweep rates, the diffusion phenomena may limit the migration of ions. Consequently, the specific capacitance value diminishes [69].
The charge storage behavior of NCM and NCM-rGO electrodes are evaluated by chronopotentiometry technique. Figure 10A and $\mathbf{B}$ indicates the charge-discharge curves at diverse current densities alternating from 1 to $5{\mathrm{~A~g}}^{-1}$ . As can be seen in the charge-discharge profile that a distinct plateau region is obtained during the progression, which representing the pseudocapacitive nature of electrodes and it is consistent with the corresponding CV results. Fig. 10C shows the charge-discharge behavior of both NCM and NCM-rGO electrodes at a current density of $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ . Interestingly, the NCM-rGO electrode exhibits longer discharge time which implies the superior specific capacitance performance. The specific capacitance from the chargedischarge profile can be reckoned by equation 2. The estimated specific capacitance of NCM and NCM-rGO electrodes are 565 and $958\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. Fascinatingly, the NCM-rGO electrode delivered the high specific capacitance, which is more reliable with the result acquired from the CV curves. The specific capacitance of newly fabricated electrodes at numerous current densities is summarized in Fig. 10D. Owing to augmentation in voltage drop and insufficient time to accomplish the redox reaction by the active material, the specific capacitance values decrease with an increase in current density [70].
The stability of the electrode material is an essential feature for commercial supercapacitor applications. The long-term cycling stability of NCM and NCM-rGO electrodes are investigated by continuous CV measurements over 5000 cycles at a high sweep rate of 100 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V~s}^{-1}$ as presented in Fig. 11A and B. It is noted from Fig. 11C that the specific capacitance gradually increases up to the initial 1000 cycles, which is possibly correlated to the activation of coated NCM materials [71]. The specific capacitance value is gradually decreased from $283~\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ to $266~\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ after 5000 cycles and affords $94\%$ of its initial capacitance. On the other hand, the specific capacitance of the NCM-rGO electrode increases during the first 500 cycles and it is continued and reached $315~\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ $289~\mathrm{Fg}{-}1$ at first cycle) and maintains $109\%$ of its initial capacitance after 5000 cycles. A similar trend is obtained and explained well in the previously reported literature [71,72,73]. The resulting stability of the NCM-rGO electrode may be owing to the following factors: (1) the rGO act as a conductive network to facilitate the fast electron transportation across the NCM rods. (2) the rGO matrix prevents aggregation of the rod-like morphology of NCM, provides the high surface area for an electrochemical redox reaction.
Fig. 12A depicts the surface micrograph of the Ni/Co-MOF/rGO composite coated on nickel foil. It is apparent from the micrograph that the fabricated electrode exhibits a rough surface with complete coverage of underlying nickel foil. The micro-rod like mof particles was visibly observed on the surface. Fig. 12B illustrates the surface micrographs of the fabricated electrode after 5000 cycles. It is inferred from the image that the micro-rod like structure of mof particles was retained even after 5000 cycles. It displays a smooth surface and a lot of pores were observed. This may owe to change in the volume of particles during cycling. These pores proficiently improve the electrode-electrolyte contact area, enhance the specific capacitance with tremendous cycling stability. Fig. 12C and D reveal the composition of the coated electrode before and after cycling. It demonstrates the presence of characteristic elements like C, O, Ni and Co. After cycling, the electrode confirms the existence of $\mathrm{\bfK}$ and concealed with crystallized KOH.
## 4. Conclusions
The intention of enhancing capacitance, we have successfully synthesized the NCM and NCM-rGO composite through a facile one-step synthesis method in a mixture of solvents. The as-prepared materials were thermally stable up to ${\sim}400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It is evident from EDS analysis that the quantity of Co and Ni exist in the NCM and NCM-rGO were ${\sim}3{:}2$ ratio, which is consistent with the experimental taken molar ratio. The maximum specific capacitance value of $1162\mathrm{F~g}^{-1}$ was achieved at a low sweep rate of $5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ for NCM-rGO composite. The emerging of rGO in the NCM might be accountable for the high specific capacitance, which creates NCM-rGO composite as an excellent candidate for electrode material in electrochemical energy storage devices.
